WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.135 --> 00:00:05.498
[MUSIC]

00:00:05.498 --> 00:00:08.563
I burst through the door and
run up the stairs.

00:00:08.563 --> 00:00:13.509
In my hands I'm clutching on to a brand
new box of general purpose Legos.

00:00:13.509 --> 00:00:15.414
I set them down on the floor.

00:00:15.414 --> 00:00:19.030
My brother scurries in behind me and
plops down next to me, we open the box.

00:00:21.480 --> 00:00:22.400
Whoa!

00:00:22.400 --> 00:00:24.465
Look at all the pieces.

00:00:24.465 --> 00:00:28.130
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; Our imaginations are racing.

00:00:28.130 --> 00:00:30.000
We dump out the box.

00:00:30.000 --> 00:00:31.930
Pieces are all over the floor.

00:00:31.930 --> 00:00:34.890
We're thinking, what are we going to make?

00:00:36.400 --> 00:00:41.000
And we start to build and we build and
we deconstruct and we build some more.

00:00:41.000 --> 00:00:42.950
Until by the end of it, the whole floor,

00:00:42.950 --> 00:00:45.950
spreading across the whole
floor is a giant Lego city.

00:00:49.090 --> 00:00:53.550
We are excited, we're looking at it with
awe, with a little bit of bewilderment.

00:00:54.650 --> 00:00:55.490
This is fun.

00:00:57.300 --> 00:01:00.910
So, I'm sure that at some
point in your childhood

00:01:00.910 --> 00:01:05.220
you've had similar feelings of awe or
bewilderment, excitement.

00:01:05.220 --> 00:01:10.150
Excitement about discovery, creating
something, or just learning something new.

00:01:10.150 --> 00:01:14.882
Whether you built a fort or
sang a new song or something of the sort.

00:01:14.882 --> 00:01:19.991
Take yourself back to that moment and
reimagine those feelings.

00:01:22.754 --> 00:01:24.727
Now fast forward.

00:01:24.727 --> 00:01:25.590
You're an adult.

00:01:27.040 --> 00:01:31.330
The specter of responsibility
washes over you like a tide.

00:01:33.040 --> 00:01:38.107
So you still have some curiosity,
but largely tracked paths and

00:01:38.107 --> 00:01:41.010
societal structures
are getting in your way.

00:01:42.470 --> 00:01:46.999
You don't really feel that youthful
sense of awe or exuberance anymore.

00:01:48.780 --> 00:01:50.240
And this is tragic.

00:01:51.980 --> 00:01:55.450
It's tragic for you, and
it's tragic for a society.

00:01:55.450 --> 00:01:59.920
You see, our ability to innovate and
create and

00:01:59.920 --> 00:02:04.334
change the world as a society is heavily
dependent on our creative ability,

00:02:04.334 --> 00:02:09.620
our imagination, our desire to build.

00:02:09.620 --> 00:02:14.500
If you look at the historical record, we
can find many examples of societies that

00:02:14.500 --> 00:02:18.736
had fallen by the wayside when
they've lost their ability to create,

00:02:18.736 --> 00:02:22.690
to innovate, to imagine.

00:02:22.690 --> 00:02:24.552
Let's take an example.

00:02:24.552 --> 00:02:28.202
Take yourself back to
the sixth century BC.

00:02:28.202 --> 00:02:31.230
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; This is the Persian Empire.

00:02:31.230 --> 00:02:33.900
You're in the capital city of Persepolis.

00:02:35.260 --> 00:02:39.130
You're looking around in amazement
at the wonderful monuments and

00:02:39.130 --> 00:02:43.950
sculptures, statues,
great architecture everywhere.

00:02:43.950 --> 00:02:47.700
You also know that you're protected by
the most innovative military in history,

00:02:49.380 --> 00:02:53.000
which is an accumulation of
people from many far off lands.

00:02:54.190 --> 00:02:58.570
And the government is innovative too,
they've created the say trap system,

00:02:58.570 --> 00:03:00.260
which is brand new and in full force.

00:03:02.060 --> 00:03:07.270
But ultimately, stagnation sets in.

00:03:07.270 --> 00:03:11.460
And within 200 years,
the Persian Empire has succumbed to

00:03:12.760 --> 00:03:16.210
the forces of other
cultures who have become

00:03:16.210 --> 00:03:20.860
stronger based on their own ability
to create, innovate, imagine.

00:03:20.860 --> 00:03:23.189
In this case,
we know that culture to be the Greeks.

00:03:24.360 --> 00:03:29.410
So we can sort of think
of this as on a graph.

00:03:29.410 --> 00:03:33.610
If we plot the importance and
influence of a society on the y axis and

00:03:33.610 --> 00:03:37.580
time on the x axis,
we sora get a bell curve sort of shape.

00:03:37.580 --> 00:03:40.150
Rise and fall of civilizations,
if you will.

00:03:40.150 --> 00:03:45.410
And if we plot innovations that
the societies are responsible for

00:03:45.410 --> 00:03:51.670
in these little red dots, most of them
are to the left side of the peak,

00:03:51.670 --> 00:03:55.590
and after the peak, not so
much innovation anymore.

00:03:55.590 --> 00:03:56.410
Coincidence?

00:03:57.590 --> 00:03:58.100
I don't think so.

00:03:59.250 --> 00:04:02.280
Take the immediate successor to Persia,
the Greeks.

00:04:03.590 --> 00:04:05.050
Very similar.

00:04:05.050 --> 00:04:09.050
We're still familiar with a lot of their
contributions to culture, even our

00:04:09.050 --> 00:04:13.660
culture today, but they still could
not retain their ability to innovate.

00:04:13.660 --> 00:04:16.340
And it keeps going.

00:04:16.340 --> 00:04:19.830
The Romans, The Byzantines,
all the way up until,

00:04:19.830 --> 00:04:22.670
take a look all the way
on the right over there.

00:04:23.980 --> 00:04:25.090
Looks pretty familiar, doesn't it?

00:04:26.140 --> 00:04:28.360
We don't want this to happen to us.

00:04:28.360 --> 00:04:30.260
Do not let this happen to us.

00:04:32.370 --> 00:04:36.760
So we just looked at this arc
of society and civilization.

00:04:36.760 --> 00:04:40.450
And remarkably it mirrors
the arc of our own lives.

00:04:40.450 --> 00:04:43.190
We talked about our experience
in childhood and adulthood.

00:04:44.920 --> 00:04:48.677
Take a moment now to try and
picture your own retirement.

00:04:48.677 --> 00:04:51.917
You've probably succeeded in a lot of the
things you wanted to accomplish in life,

00:04:51.917 --> 00:04:53.870
feeling pretty good about yourself.

00:04:53.870 --> 00:04:57.100
Maybe you've got a house off in
the woods or on the beach somewhere.

00:04:58.690 --> 00:05:02.267
But the reality of it is,
is that depression

00:05:02.267 --> 00:05:06.919
actually increases by 40%
in when Americans retire.

00:05:06.919 --> 00:05:13.532
This is largely due the fact that our job
is heavily tied to our creative purpose.

00:05:16.017 --> 00:05:23.490
Additionally, suicide rates double for
Americans of retirement age, 65 to 85.

00:05:23.490 --> 00:05:27.660
This all sounds a bit morbid but
at a large and

00:05:27.660 --> 00:05:31.930
small scale,
the failure to create is fatal.

00:05:34.110 --> 00:05:38.320
Now, I want to tease out this
connection between society and

00:05:38.320 --> 00:05:39.620
the individual a little bit more.

00:05:40.700 --> 00:05:44.010
Obviously individuals drive society.

00:05:44.010 --> 00:05:48.010
And in a lot of cases these
individuals are people

00:05:48.010 --> 00:05:52.860
who are committed and motivated and
working towards a goal.

00:05:54.030 --> 00:05:57.700
But we tend to remember a lot
of people who simply achieve and

00:05:57.700 --> 00:05:59.380
create because that's
what they like to do.

00:05:59.380 --> 00:06:01.510
It's something that really speaks to them.

00:06:03.240 --> 00:06:07.368
People like this might
include Benjamin Franklin,

00:06:07.368 --> 00:06:12.354
Archimedes, Leonardo Da Vinci,
these guys are luminaries.

00:06:12.354 --> 00:06:18.793
But it can't just be up to a small number
of people to drive society forward,

00:06:18.793 --> 00:06:22.830
or to innovate, create,
it's up to all of us.

00:06:24.770 --> 00:06:27.090
Let's take a local example.

00:06:27.090 --> 00:06:29.851
So back when I was working
with my robotics lab,

00:06:29.851 --> 00:06:33.935
one of the guys in the lab would come
in with this tub of Legos every day.

00:06:33.935 --> 00:06:36.536
This is a 27 years old guy,
carrying his Legos.

00:06:36.536 --> 00:06:40.390
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; And he'd build things with on his desk.

00:06:40.390 --> 00:06:42.155
What are you doing?

00:06:42.155 --> 00:06:46.660
But those ended up being prototypes for
some of the best robots that he built.

00:06:46.660 --> 00:06:51.350
And he was unequivocally the best
design engineer in our lab.

00:06:52.650 --> 00:06:57.130
He never lost his inner kid, he never lost
his ability to create and to innovate.

00:06:59.470 --> 00:07:03.580
For me, my own experience with
building is really important to me.

00:07:04.890 --> 00:07:09.070
My parents used to tell me when I
was growing up, do what you enjoy.

00:07:09.070 --> 00:07:13.332
And for me, it was building Lego towns,
like the one I was talking about.

00:07:13.332 --> 00:07:15.670
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; So I wanted to just keep doing that.

00:07:15.670 --> 00:07:19.080
But you get older, and
there’s no reason that this should stop.

00:07:19.080 --> 00:07:24.160
No reason innovating and creating and
just having that sense of awe should stop.

00:07:24.160 --> 00:07:26.550
In high school,
I was part of my first robotics team and

00:07:26.550 --> 00:07:28.990
we built this robot here.

00:07:28.990 --> 00:07:35.050
And I've since become an actual robotics
engineer, and this robot is one that

00:07:35.050 --> 00:07:39.880
we made for the DARPA Robotics Challenge,
for disaster recovery scenarios.

00:07:40.940 --> 00:07:42.630
This robot is meant to save lives.

00:07:44.560 --> 00:07:48.670
So this is all very,
this is inspiring to me,

00:07:48.670 --> 00:07:50.360
it's something that means
a great deal to me.

00:07:51.630 --> 00:07:55.980
And I found there's almost sort of
current of inspiration and

00:07:55.980 --> 00:08:00.040
fulfillment that skirting beneath
the surface of our lives.

00:08:00.040 --> 00:08:03.681
And we just need to dig
a little bit to tap into it.

00:08:06.444 --> 00:08:09.881
Now for me I get that through building and
creating, but

00:08:09.881 --> 00:08:11.720
we all should seek to feel it.

00:08:13.860 --> 00:08:16.950
Immerse yourself in creation and
discovery.

00:08:16.950 --> 00:08:21.840
The best way to avoid personal stagnation,

00:08:21.840 --> 00:08:28.720
by extension societal stagnation is to,
and to reach a higher state of fulfilment.

00:08:29.740 --> 00:08:32.610
Is to dig into your passions and create.

00:08:34.180 --> 00:08:38.150
Avoid the external,
the traps of external validation,

00:08:38.150 --> 00:08:42.580
be it status, money, power and
think about what you love.

00:08:45.750 --> 00:08:46.890
When you go to sleep tonight and

00:08:46.890 --> 00:08:50.540
put your head on the pillow, think
about what you did today that was new.

00:08:52.950 --> 00:08:54.280
What did, how did that make you feel?

00:08:55.810 --> 00:08:58.708
What will you create tomorrow?

00:08:58.708 --> 00:09:01.007
What will be new?

00:09:01.007 --> 00:09:04.290
Pitch, what will be new?

00:09:04.290 --> 00:09:05.900
JD, what are you going to create?

00:09:07.560 --> 00:09:11.548
And do it, tap into your inner current.

00:09:11.548 --> 00:09:16.930
You depend on it and
society depends on it.

00:09:16.930 --> 00:09:17.906
Thank you.

00:09:17.906 --> 00:09:22.224
&gt;&gt; [APPLAUSE]

00:09:22.224 --> 00:09:26.699
[MUSIC]

